I don't think any of this precludes use of CC0 for the data because it's all "to the extent possible." I'd have to double check, but I don't think use of CC0 implies a claim of any ownership. Which is to say, we can use CC0 but that doesn't mean someone else couldn't still claim infringement.

For the code, I'd say that if we use CC0 for the data then we should use that too for the code for the sake of simplicity. One license per repo. :)

Here's what I did. There are 10,000+ notes in the file, so I parsed them into a hierarchical list in JSON format and put them on my skydrive folder at :

https://skydrive.live.com/redir?resid=D2044F735FC6A41D!1356&authkey=!AF0xohIQkoSJN8I&ithint=file%2c.zip

The json schema is based on these classes:

```
 class Congress {
    public String congress;
    public String date;
    public ArrayList<Session> sessions;
}
class Session {
    public String session;
    public String date;
    public ArrayList<State> states;
}
class State {
    public String state;
    public ArrayList<Chamber> chambers;
}
class Chamber {
    public String chamber;
    public ArrayList<Note> notes;
}
public class Note {
    public String item;
    public String note;
    public int line;
}
```

The "action" takes place in the item field and the note field. The item is typically a name. The name will be in the hierarchy under congress, session, state, and chamber. The note is the footnote. Basically, I only parsed out the footnotes, but there is also the congress dates range and the session dates range. I also included a debugfile.txt. It has each of the lines as they came out of the parser, preceded with a line number. In the json file, each item has a line number, so it matches the debug file. So, if there are questions or problems, you can look in the debug file. There are questions an problems, but it looks like the parser has pretty good accuracy. The things I noticed where:

1) a few of the notes are "kinda funny"
2) There seems to be some footnotes in the debugfile. I think that they didn't get picked out as footnotes by word, so they were probably left out. It only seemed to happen early on. It's easy enough to find them, they seem to be the only lines in the debugfile that starts with a number (after the line number). So a quick secondary parser should pick those up, but they will have to be matched by hand, so you will need to check the original PDF file. There seems to be somewhere between 50 and 100 but I didn't look very close.
3) The names for the leadership positions has some troubles. I couldn't see any obvious way to fix them beyond what I could do. 
4) The java code is up on github at  https://github.com/karlnicholas/parse-gpo-cdoc

That's about it. I need to work other things at the moment so I can't try to sort out the python or merging part at this time.

Hope this helps. Let me know what you think.

